Found seven issues. Phase 16 should not be considered gated complete.

1. [P1] Provider validation messages can leak secrets through the API.  
   [registry.py:338](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/shared/providers_common/registry.py:338) copies provider-authored `message` values verbatim into `ValidationIssue`; [routes.py:84](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/providers/routes.py:84) serializes them unchanged. A provider returning `{"message": "rejected sk-secret"}` exposes that secret through validation, settings-save, and selection responses.

2. [P1] Provider lifecycle exceptions are written unredacted to persistent logs.  
   Raw exceptions reach Loguru from validation, health, construction, and shutdown paths at [registry.py:328](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/shared/providers_common/registry.py:328), line 361, line 434, and line 503. I confirmed `api_key=sk-review-secret` appears verbatim in both health and construction logs. This directly contradicts the recorded “secrets never appear in logs” gate.

3. [P1] Archives copy referenced artifacts without inspecting or redacting them.  
   [project_archive.py:215](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/workflows/project_archive.py:215) copies artifact bytes unchanged. A referenced JSON artifact containing `{"api_key":"sk-review-archive-secret"}` retained the secret in the resulting ZIP. The new test only dirties workflow/execution metadata, not artifact contents.

4. [P2] Phase 16 breaks the existing Chromium API contract.  
   [app.py:210](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/app.py:210) removed the historical default target, so `POST /api/chromium/activate-tab {}` changed from activating Gemini to HTTP 400. The health response changed from top-level `gemini`/`grok` fields to `extensions` at line 235, and focus results moved under `results` at line 246. Current frontend code was updated, but old clients are incompatible despite the “old APIs remain compatible” criterion.

5. [P2] The legacy Pipeline UI remains concretely tied to the two built-in TTS providers.  
   [usePipelineForm.js:80](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/frontend/src/features/pipeline/composables/usePipelineForm.js:80) fetches fixed Kokoro/Inworld voice lists, while lines 110–122 route every non-Inworld provider through Kokoro state. [PipelinePage.vue:118](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/frontend/src/features/pipeline/views/PipelinePage.vue:118) similarly dispatches Inworld specially and treats every other provider as Kokoro. A third conforming TTS package therefore cannot work correctly in this UI without central edits.

6. [P2] Scaffold failure is not atomic when the first write fails.  
   [scaffold.py:801](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/shared/providers_common/scaffold.py:801) creates directories before writing, but cleanup at line 819 only considers parents of successfully created files. Simulating failure on the first `write_text()` leaves the new provider directory behind, violating step 16.2’s atomic-failure requirement.

7. [P2] Valid CLI labels can generate syntactically invalid packages.  
   User-provided labels are interpolated directly into Python string literals at [scaffold.py:160](/D:/@Workspace/@Development/@Scripts/@Python/ScriptToScene-Studio-V2/studio/shared/providers_common/scaffold.py:160). Scaffolding with `--label 'Bob "quoted" TTS'` succeeds but produces a `manifest.py` that fails compilation; labels containing newlines fail similarly.

All declared deterministic gates still pass: backend `1088 passed, 12 skipped`, frontend `280 passed`, production build successful, and documentation drift check successful. The worktree remains clean. These results demonstrate missing adversarial coverage rather than gate health.
